WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.610
- Hi, everybody and welcome.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:06.220
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:09.410
I'm Stephen Pitti, Professor
of History, American Studies,

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.290
and Ethnicity Race Migration here at Yale

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:12.857
and Director of Yale Center
for the Study of Race,

00:00:12.857 --> 00:00:15.720
Indigeneity, and Transnational Migration.

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:18.900
I'm delighted today to host this event

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.790
with three terrific artists
who've been fellows,

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.540
this semester at Yale's RITM Center.

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.560
They were selected from an amazing

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.820
international pool of applicants,

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:30.470
and we've been thrilled in recent months

00:00:30.470 --> 00:00:33.640
to play a small role in helping
them to advance their work.

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:36.470
We're grateful for the ways
in which they've contributed

00:00:36.470 --> 00:00:38.930
to our campus community over time

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.490
and excited about what lies ahead.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:43.730
So we expect this event will run

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:45.840
a little bit more than an hour.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.550
I'll introduce our three artists briefly,

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.240
and then each of them
will share their work

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.223
for roughly 10 or 15 minutes.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.020
Fellows, I'll send you a direct
message at minute 15 or so

00:00:57.020 --> 00:00:58.440
just to remind you to wrap up.

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.710
So please keep your eye on the messages.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:04.560
And afterwards, after
they've finished talking,

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:06.820
I'll invite the artists
to talk with one another

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.200
if they'd like to do that.

00:01:08.200 --> 00:01:10.920
And maybe I'll have a
question or two for them,

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.070
but we're also hoping that
members of the audience

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:15.900
will send in questions
via the Q&amp;A function.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.440
So if you send a comment or
a question there in the Q&amp;A,

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:23.040
I'll do my best to then
share it with the panelists

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:25.030
towards the end of our time together.

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:29.460
I wanna thank our center
staff, Nicole Edwards,

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.920
Maggie Katz, and Victoria Stone-Cadena

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:34.490
for their hard work in
running this Fellows Program

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.500
and so much else and for
making this event happen today.

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:42.400
Let me now introduce Sonia,
and Rodney, and akua quickly,

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.420
and then I'll get out of the way.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.690
Sonia E. Barrett performs
composites of plants,

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:53.260
animals, elements and people
to create interventions

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.660
that presence their objectification
and commodification,

00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.870
she also thinks about
how to change perceptions

00:01:58.870 --> 00:02:03.770
of phenomena in nature that are a given.

00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:05.930
The work seeks to create new questions

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:07.920
where there was a kind of certainty

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.340
that has to do with the hegemony

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:11.343
of normative wester European values.

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:13.920
Her work unpacks the boundaries

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.660
between the determined and the determining

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.490
with a focus on race and gender.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:20.940
She makes sculptural works

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:23.420
so she can run her
hands along the fissures

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.470
and manifest strategies for multiple

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.610
compatible existences and mourn.

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:31.920
Her sculptural practice
includes placemaking

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.940
with a view to assembling communities

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:38.110
under the threat of
climate to reclaim space

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:41.020
as well as instituting permanently.

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.930
Sonia is a MacDowell fellow

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.990
and has been recognized
by the Premio Ora prize,

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.740
New York Art-Slant showcase for sculpture

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:50.933
and the Neo Art Prize.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.720
She has exhibited by the
National Gallery of Jamaica,

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.600
32 degrees East Gallery, Kampala, Uganda,

00:02:58.600 --> 00:03:02.350
the Heinrich Böll Institute
Germany, the British Library,

00:03:02.350 --> 00:03:04.750
the Museum of Derby, and
the Kunsthaus Nürnberg.

00:03:05.740 --> 00:03:08.400
Her work has been shown
at a number of galleries

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:13.400
including the OCCCA
California, the NGBK Berlin,

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:17.260
Tete Berlin, The Format
Contemporary in Milan and Basel

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:21.080
and the Rosenwald Wolf
Gallery, Philadelphia.

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:23.050
Her works have been published

00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:25.890
in the International Review
of African American Art,

00:03:25.890 --> 00:03:30.060
Black History 365 Journal,
Kunstforum International,

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.060
Protocollum, ELSE Journal and
the Contemporary and Platform,

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:38.270
and British Art Studies Journal.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.520
She is a co-initiator
of the AIPCC in Bavaria.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.050
Thank you Sonia.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.190
Rodney Gomez is a poet and urbanist

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:49.580
whose work explores the
intersection of place,

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:52.880
culture, and history in the
South Texas Borderlands.

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.037
Rodney has a particular
interest in Latinx poetry,

00:03:56.037 --> 00:03:59.100
art phrases, and visual poetry.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.650
His collections include
"Arsenal With Praise Song",

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:06.590
recipient of the Helen
C. Smith Memorial Award

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:10.407
for best poetry book from the
Texas Institute of Letters.

00:04:10.407 --> 00:04:12.260
"Geographic Tongue",

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:16.017
winner of the Pleiades
Press Visual Poetry Series.

00:04:16.017 --> 00:04:21.017
"Ceremony of Sand" winner of
the Writers' League of Texas

00:04:21.332 --> 00:04:23.820
and Discovery Prize, and
"Citizens of the Mausoleum"

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.260
finalist for the John A. Robertson Award.

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:31.370
His work appears in
Poetry, New England Review,

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:35.000
The Gettysburg Review,
Denver Quarterly, Rattle,

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:38.370
North American Review,
Verse Daily, Poetry Daily,

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:39.610
and other journals.

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.370
His chapbook, "Mouth
Filled Night" was awarded

00:04:42.370 --> 00:04:43.870
the Drinking Gourd Prize

00:04:43.870 --> 00:04:45.580
from Northwestern University's Poetry

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:47.200
and Poetics Colloquium.

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:49.730
He's the recipient of the Editor's Prize

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:54.300
from the Rhino Magazine, the
Gloria Anzaldúa Poetry Prize,

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.050
the Rania Royal Prize, and
the Orison Anthology Award.

00:04:58.050 --> 00:05:00.483
A member of the Macondo Writers' Workshop.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:05.930
Gomez was the 2020, 2021 poet
Laureate of McAllen, Texas,

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:08.853
and received an Academy of
American Poets Fellowship.

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:13.700
He serves as advisory
editor for FlowerSong Press,

00:05:13.700 --> 00:05:15.960
and a board member of Newfound,

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.330
a nonprofit publisher
that explores how place

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:21.343
shapes identity, imagination,
and understanding.

00:05:22.230 --> 00:05:25.160
He works as executive director
of parking and transportation

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:27.380
at the University of
Texas Rio Grande Valley,

00:05:27.380 --> 00:05:29.060
and is a certified planner,

00:05:29.060 --> 00:05:32.180
transportation planner,
and project manager.

00:05:32.180 --> 00:05:34.407
Thank you for being here Rodney.

00:05:34.407 --> 00:05:37.280
akua naru is a hip hop artist,

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.210
poet, producer, lecturer, and activist.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:43.500
Who's been called "Toni
Morrison of hip hop."

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.220
Her body of work like Morrisons

00:05:45.220 --> 00:05:47.420
always holds black folk at its center.

00:05:47.420 --> 00:05:50.610
A sonic love letter
sprawled across four albums

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.103
and countless features.

00:05:53.260 --> 00:05:55.347
She released her debut album,

00:05:55.347 --> 00:06:00.227
"The Journey aflame" followed
by a live version in 2012,

00:06:00.227 --> 00:06:02.706
"live &amp; aflame sessions."

00:06:02.706 --> 00:06:06.517
naru followed up this
release with two more albums,

00:06:06.517 --> 00:06:11.517
"The Miners Canary," 2015
and "The Blackest Joy," 2018.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:14.690
She's performed hundreds of
shows in more than 50 countries

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:17.900
across five continents
with her 6-piece band.

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:22.623
She's recorded with Tony Allen,
Eric Benét, Angelique Kidjo,

00:06:22.623 --> 00:06:25.340
Tuneyards, Questlove,
Georgia Anne Muldrow,

00:06:25.340 --> 00:06:28.838
Christian Scott, Rah
Digga, Mulatu Astatke,

00:06:28.838 --> 00:06:33.540
Bernard Purdie, sorry,
Cody ChesnuTT, and more.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.040
She's a has a series of
ongoing collaborations

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:40.250
with Canadian filmmaker and
photographer Gregory Colbert.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:44.350
In 2021, she voiced the
"Save the Night" commercial

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:47.400
for German company, Jägermeister.

00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:49.530
Towards a larger vision of empowerment,

00:06:49.530 --> 00:06:52.420
she's hosted workshops,
healing circles, lectures,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:55.210
and participated on
panels that explore topics

00:06:55.210 --> 00:06:58.560
such as African Spiritualties
in relation to hip hop

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.980
and sexism in the hip hop industry.

00:07:00.980 --> 00:07:02.880
She's lectured at many, many universities

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.010
around the world and has been a fellow

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.770
at both Harvard and Brown Universities.

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:11.920
She is also the founder and
artistic director of The Keeper,

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:13.680
the first comprehensive archive

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:16.010
focusing on women's contributions

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:19.290
to five decades of hip
hop music and culture.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.490
So with that, I thank you for
being here all three of you.

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:25.190
And I'm gonna turn this over,
I think to Sonia, Sonia.

00:07:29.220 --> 00:07:30.730
- Hello, thank you for introduction.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:33.440
It's just wonderful to be
here and in the company of

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:35.403
such amazing artists as well.

00:07:38.827 --> 00:07:41.647
I'm just going to share my screen.

00:07:44.920 --> 00:07:49.920
So I'm just going touch on
just about three things.

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:56.330
Firstly, I hope everyone can
see that's the screen, great.

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:57.920
The premise of my work,

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.630
just one large or two large
installations I've done.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:03.510
And then one work that's in progress

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:04.973
since I've been at Yale.

00:08:12.121 --> 00:08:14.954
So to just really start with this,

00:08:17.693 --> 00:08:19.680
my work is really all about

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:21.400
inter-species object objectification.

00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:24.290
So I'm really thinking
about how it's a given that

00:08:24.290 --> 00:08:27.580
our everyday existence
is like predicated on

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:30.890
the depletion of animal
and mineral life on earth,

00:08:30.890 --> 00:08:33.890
but also that there are groups of laborers

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.057
who are unrecognized unenumerated,

00:08:36.057 --> 00:08:37.173
and are essential to the status quo,

00:08:37.173 --> 00:08:41.190
they're really holding
everything up so to speak.

00:08:41.190 --> 00:08:44.427
And in those terms I'm thinking about

00:08:44.427 --> 00:08:46.320
the history of migration,

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:49.859
black and brown peoples,
caregivers, historical slavery,

00:08:49.859 --> 00:08:53.182
descendants, historical
descendants of historical slaves,

00:08:53.182 --> 00:08:55.050
and also modern day slavery.

00:08:56.770 --> 00:08:59.130
This is like really the premise of my work

00:08:59.130 --> 00:09:01.930
and modern day slavery often comes up

00:09:01.930 --> 00:09:04.640
with a lot of resistance
and just some figures here

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.493
about modern day slavery.

00:09:08.420 --> 00:09:11.163
It's that it's a real phenomena.

00:09:15.740 --> 00:09:18.047
The next thing I'd like to share is the,

00:09:22.670 --> 00:09:27.670
so basically the modern
day slavery is really it's,

00:09:27.879 --> 00:09:30.413
it's a very intimate space.

00:09:30.413 --> 00:09:33.390
Modern day slaves handle all
the food we put in our mouths,

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:35.290
while the laboring in
the farming industry,

00:09:35.290 --> 00:09:37.490
they highlight Italian
in the sex industry.

00:09:37.490 --> 00:09:42.250
They deal with our bodily
fluid, blood, fluid, mucus

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:43.570
in caregiving roles.

00:09:43.570 --> 00:09:46.920
It's a very intimate content
that's often overlooked.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:48.940
So for me to really think
about the objectification

00:09:48.940 --> 00:09:51.900
of black and brown people
and people as furniture.

00:09:51.900 --> 00:09:54.950
And if you think about the
chair you are sitting on now,

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:56.300
it's right into the contact,

00:09:56.300 --> 00:09:57.973
it's holding your entire weight.

00:09:58.940 --> 00:10:01.640
But you probably didn't even
see it when you sat down.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.930
There's a history of looking at furniture

00:10:08.930 --> 00:10:11.610
in art and it's not...

00:10:13.050 --> 00:10:15.750
Many people have looked
at furniture and art

00:10:15.750 --> 00:10:17.780
and looking at the
history of furniture art,

00:10:17.780 --> 00:10:19.540
I discovered there's some art,

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.050
some furniture has already
got like a live element,

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:23.680
it's got a line foot in it.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:25.840
This is the queen and
style it's been revived

00:10:25.840 --> 00:10:27.310
the most times in Europe.

00:10:27.310 --> 00:10:29.550
It's the most revived
style of you in furniture.

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:30.383
And in my practice,

00:10:30.383 --> 00:10:33.610
I started replacing that
furniture with my own body.

00:10:33.610 --> 00:10:36.219
And discovered that I
didn't actually need a prop,

00:10:36.219 --> 00:10:40.810
my body looked already looked
like that kind of line foot.

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:43.010
So in making that foot,

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:48.010
I could begin to think about
objectification of my own body.

00:10:48.140 --> 00:10:51.588
This was a surreal work I
made a response to Fanon,

00:10:51.588 --> 00:10:54.670
his quote where he speaks about how

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:59.120
we have to stop our beauty
slumber in the first world

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:01.930
and wake up from a beauty
slumber in the first world

00:11:01.930 --> 00:11:05.910
and respond to the needs of
those in the third world.

00:11:05.910 --> 00:11:09.220
And so this is, being the
bed and getting in the bed.

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:11.963
I sort off the leg of the
bed and put my arm there

00:11:11.963 --> 00:11:14.810
so that I was the bed and
tried to get into the bed

00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:17.830
to try and problematize
this idea of splity slumber

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:22.360
oppression and define
this kind of brown space

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:25.453
of being in between those
two so called worlds.

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:30.090
I work with furniture and
I keep thinking about it

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:35.090
again and again, the work that
I'm doing is to corpse pop.

00:11:35.610 --> 00:11:40.020
So to expand what part is for
living, what's worth mourning.

00:11:40.020 --> 00:11:43.693
These are American table
legs, 18th century.

00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:46.970
I'm also trying to think
about how can we mourn

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:50.530
maybe an ore as well as a brown body,

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:53.090
as well as a tree and a
plant, everything together

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:54.680
and perhaps this is one body

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.740
and that we shouldn't think
about them as separate things.

00:11:57.740 --> 00:12:02.210
I'm thinking about
violation and the witnessing

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:05.660
the violation as a continuum,
as a holding space,

00:12:05.660 --> 00:12:08.780
Kevin Bales talks about how
slave holding, not slave owning.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:13.780
I'm asking you to be
detective and think about who,

00:12:14.690 --> 00:12:17.173
what the crime is, bloodless crime scene.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:22.133
This is a gentleman's
smoking chair from a club,

00:12:23.631 --> 00:12:25.680
and also trying to strategize,

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:27.880
how can we move from objectification?

00:12:27.880 --> 00:12:32.350
And this is one way in that
we can rest at the space

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:34.320
of oppression rather than leaving.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.130
So this resting, this
chair articulated itself,

00:12:37.130 --> 00:12:38.780
we wanted to rest.

00:12:38.780 --> 00:12:40.080
Also, we can collectivize,

00:12:40.946 --> 00:12:44.497
in collectivizing even if
we hold the same viewpoint,

00:12:45.730 --> 00:12:48.900
we can disrupt this space of consumption.

00:12:48.900 --> 00:12:50.020
So it used to seat 12

00:12:50.020 --> 00:12:53.730
and now nobody can sit at
this chair at this table.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:55.190
And it also illustrates the problem

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:57.910
in that there's a kind
of holding space as well.

00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:59.760
And it's like looking into
the bowels of maybe a ship

00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:00.943
or a holding space.

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:05.640
I have so much heartache
and headache with this work.

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:09.510
The most like a key
headache happened after

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:11.840
I'd done my last research in New Orleans.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:13.980
And I finished being at a plantation

00:13:13.980 --> 00:13:16.760
and there was actually
headache tablets in the museum.

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:19.160
And I felt, oh my God, I can't
wait to leave this space.

00:13:19.160 --> 00:13:22.420
I can't wait to go back to London to deal,

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:24.610
just to get out of this plantation space.

00:13:24.610 --> 00:13:26.850
And then I realized that's
actually crazy because

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:31.660
London is really an
epicenter of all of this.

00:13:31.660 --> 00:13:35.610
So geography, isn't the next
thing I started to tackle

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:40.440
because London is seen as
separate from New Orleans,

00:13:40.440 --> 00:13:43.020
it's seen separate from
the Caribbean or Africa.

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:46.440
And so I started to braid those spaces.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:49.360
So those maps from those
spaces of triangle spade.

00:13:49.360 --> 00:13:52.913
So this is London Corn Road,
that's the river Thames here.

00:13:54.000 --> 00:13:57.200
This is New Orleans
focusing on Congress Square,

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:59.733
dreadlocked with all those other places.

00:14:00.810 --> 00:14:03.580
And again, this is map of Jamaica.

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:05.330
And then something happened in (mumbles)

