You know what I mean?

00:57:15.423 --> 00:57:17.850
To like pull, pull myself out of it,

00:57:17.850 --> 00:57:21.220
interface with reality
as a sane human being,

00:57:21.220 --> 00:57:22.053
you know what I mean?

00:57:22.053 --> 00:57:25.253
So this is kind of directed at everyone.

00:57:26.230 --> 00:57:28.043
You know whoever wants to enter.

00:57:31.630 --> 00:57:33.003
- I'm happy, I'm happy to take that,

00:57:33.003 --> 00:57:35.700
just to respond quickly.

00:57:35.700 --> 00:57:39.120
I feel so there to be very practical,

00:57:39.120 --> 00:57:43.630
like so really helpful is to have a studio

00:57:43.630 --> 00:57:45.070
so that you have a distance between

00:57:45.070 --> 00:57:47.482
what you are doing and
what you are making.

00:57:47.482 --> 00:57:51.280
And currently I don't have that.

00:57:51.280 --> 00:57:53.690
So I'm literally living with my work.

00:57:53.690 --> 00:57:58.690
It makes the work more
personal, but it's very hard.

00:58:01.540 --> 00:58:05.560
I work collectively in
spaces that are overwhelming,

00:58:05.560 --> 00:58:09.250
for example, to enter the
Royal Geographic Society

00:58:09.250 --> 00:58:12.320
headquarters in London, in the map room,

00:58:12.320 --> 00:58:13.933
where all those map makers left,

00:58:14.850 --> 00:58:17.910
I started a collective
called it the map collective

00:58:17.910 --> 00:58:21.170
because I knew it was just too
much for me to do on my own.

00:58:21.170 --> 00:58:24.640
So working collectively
is a strategy for me.

00:58:24.640 --> 00:58:27.600
I'm also a member of a collective called

00:58:27.600 --> 00:58:29.146
What the Hell She doin?

00:58:29.146 --> 00:58:31.110
And we're women from across the...

00:58:31.110 --> 00:58:32.470
Black women from across,

00:58:32.470 --> 00:58:34.570
all visual artists from across the

00:58:34.570 --> 00:58:36.720
African continent and its DSPOs.

00:58:36.720 --> 00:58:40.650
And what we have in
common is that people say,

00:58:40.650 --> 00:58:42.000
what the hell is she doing?

00:58:43.026 --> 00:58:47.340
So that's the support network that I have.

00:58:47.340 --> 00:58:52.340
And the other thing is salt
bath, lots of bath and salt,

00:58:52.840 --> 00:58:56.220
especially after going into places with,

00:58:56.220 --> 00:58:57.820
or dealing or handling

00:59:00.590 --> 00:59:03.840
these kind of bodies
that I feel and handling,

00:59:03.840 --> 00:59:06.050
after handling that or
going into these spaces.

00:59:06.050 --> 00:59:07.860
Some of the spaces that
Rodney was talking about,

00:59:07.860 --> 00:59:11.380
how they're resident like
even like the paneling,

00:59:11.380 --> 00:59:14.050
like salt bath afterwards.

00:59:14.050 --> 00:59:19.050
So those are the practical
ways that I navigate this,

00:59:19.140 --> 00:59:22.053
but very helpful also is to able to stop,

00:59:24.530 --> 00:59:29.210
which can't always happen,
but that's also very helpful.

00:59:29.210 --> 00:59:32.990
But I mean, of course this a
fellowship like this is just,

00:59:32.990 --> 00:59:34.920
an amazing catalyst and enabling

00:59:34.920 --> 00:59:36.973
so many other kind of things.

00:59:42.253 --> 00:59:44.630
Those are my strategies, really.

00:59:47.640 --> 00:59:50.793
- Yeah, just to echo
some of what Sonia said,

00:59:51.660 --> 00:59:56.660
for me, I work or try to work
with other poets and writers

00:59:57.810 --> 00:59:59.520
and try to promote them
as much as possible.

00:59:59.520 --> 01:00:02.200
So I work closely with FlowerSong Press,

01:00:02.200 --> 01:00:05.930
which is a small indie
press in South Texas

01:00:05.930 --> 01:00:10.730
that puts out a lot of
voices from people of color.

01:00:10.730 --> 01:00:15.260
We publish a lot of poetry and fiction.

01:00:15.260 --> 01:00:20.260
And so trying to find new
voices to put on to the world,

01:00:21.100 --> 01:00:24.510
I think is important to help others,

01:00:24.510 --> 01:00:26.789
get their voice out out there.

01:00:26.789 --> 01:00:29.270
I do pretty much the same
kind of work with Newfound,

01:00:29.270 --> 01:00:32.860
which is a press that has
a little bit different aim,

01:00:32.860 --> 01:00:34.040
but is also interested

01:00:34.040 --> 01:00:37.253
in promoting voices of color primarily.

01:00:38.190 --> 01:00:42.250
And then, well, I have a full-time job.

01:00:42.250 --> 01:00:45.920
And so that sort of keeps me
grounded in the world I guess,

01:00:45.920 --> 01:00:49.910
and not get carried into
what sometimes can be very

01:00:49.910 --> 01:00:54.193
depressing work in the research.

01:00:55.906 --> 01:00:58.550
'Cause it is very depressing.

01:00:58.550 --> 01:01:00.170
I mean, it's, there's no way around that.

01:01:00.170 --> 01:01:04.720
It's just and knowing
that, we're sort of at the,

01:01:04.720 --> 01:01:08.000
at the knife edge in our
society in a lot of ways.

01:01:08.000 --> 01:01:11.750
And we could sort of quickly devolve into,

01:01:11.750 --> 01:01:13.280
into that state of exception

01:01:13.280 --> 01:01:14.720
that sort of existed in the past,

01:01:14.720 --> 01:01:16.920
if we aren't already
there in a lot of ways.

01:01:17.780 --> 01:01:21.670
But yeah, in my work it's at a university

01:01:21.670 --> 01:01:23.640
and so I'm helping students,

01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:28.040
a lot of them first
generation students who,

01:01:28.040 --> 01:01:31.767
are sort of navigating, the
university and college life

01:01:31.767 --> 01:01:33.670
and just trying to make it in the world

01:01:33.670 --> 01:01:36.130
and trying to figure themselves out.

01:01:36.130 --> 01:01:38.933
So I think that's good 'cause I sort of,

01:01:39.860 --> 01:01:42.220
I'm putting my energy into that primarily,

01:01:42.220 --> 01:01:44.700
full time and then the poetry sort of

01:01:44.700 --> 01:01:46.310
is something else that I do.

01:01:46.310 --> 01:01:48.350
So I think it's important
to be grounded in something

01:01:48.350 --> 01:01:52.693
where I'm helping somebody
else get along in the world.

01:01:56.500 --> 01:01:58.670
- akua, would you like to talk
about the question yourself

01:01:58.670 --> 01:02:01.470
that you raised for a second?

01:02:01.470 --> 01:02:05.750
- You know, it's a
struggle in all honesty.

01:02:05.750 --> 01:02:08.510
I mean there are times in
which, songs that I've written,

01:02:08.510 --> 01:02:10.483
'cause I have to do a lot of research.

01:02:11.400 --> 01:02:13.260
You know what I mean for
me to write, for example,

01:02:13.260 --> 01:02:17.173
that song that I read
or performed a reciter

01:02:17.173 --> 01:02:18.483
or what have you,

01:02:19.360 --> 01:02:23.580
I had to do a lot of research
'cause I wanna be accurate

01:02:23.580 --> 01:02:27.320
in the telling of this account, right?

01:02:27.320 --> 01:02:32.110
And it's also like talking
to people in the family,

01:02:32.110 --> 01:02:36.390
like there's so much
to learn in the kitchen

01:02:36.390 --> 01:02:39.280
and conversations with
other women in the family,

01:02:39.280 --> 01:02:42.680
it passed down even at a funeral,

01:02:42.680 --> 01:02:45.250
you find out all these
other new information

01:02:45.250 --> 01:02:48.850
you didn't know about something
that changes everything.

01:02:48.850 --> 01:02:53.330
So I don't I guess it
depends on what it is.

01:02:53.330 --> 01:02:55.437
I mean there are times in
which I've written things

01:02:55.437 --> 01:02:57.890
and I couldn't get outta bed for days.

01:02:57.890 --> 01:03:02.890
It was that heavy, it was
that hard but community,

01:03:04.100 --> 01:03:06.382
and it was just kind
of like basically what,

01:03:06.382 --> 01:03:08.445
what I heard is I think that

01:03:08.445 --> 01:03:11.623
there's so much power and community and,

01:03:13.611 --> 01:03:16.620
and being held by folks who understand

01:03:16.620 --> 01:03:19.300
where there isn't much to be explained,

01:03:19.300 --> 01:03:21.580
'cause it's so exhausting,

01:03:21.580 --> 01:03:24.227
but just being held by
those who understand

01:03:24.227 --> 01:03:29.053
has really helped me, you know.

01:03:32.640 --> 01:03:33.473
- Thank you.

01:03:35.620 --> 01:03:36.920
One of the other things on my mind

01:03:36.920 --> 01:03:38.390
as all of you were talking was

01:03:38.390 --> 01:03:41.490
the question of being
readers and researchers,

01:03:41.490 --> 01:03:42.760
which I think I heard in different ways

01:03:42.760 --> 01:03:45.276
each of you talk about,
and I just, I don't...

01:03:45.276 --> 01:03:47.480
I could ask a very specific
question about this,

01:03:47.480 --> 01:03:50.170
but I'd rather just raise this
as something for discussion.

01:03:50.170 --> 01:03:52.193
I mean, Sonia you talked
about funnel and for example,

01:03:52.193 --> 01:03:55.110
akua you were just talking
about the research,

01:03:55.110 --> 01:03:57.810
Rodney you for example, were
talking about La Matanza

01:03:57.810 --> 01:04:02.560
and your relationship to
that period or set of events,

01:04:02.560 --> 01:04:05.030
which of course you would
know about through reading

01:04:05.030 --> 01:04:07.710
maybe in other ways but
certainly through reading.

01:04:07.710 --> 01:04:10.990
So I guess I just wanted to
raise this for discussion.

01:04:10.990 --> 01:04:12.140
If there's anything to say about it,

01:04:12.140 --> 01:04:15.200
how you think about yourself
as readers, as researchers,

01:04:15.200 --> 01:04:16.800
why and what you read.

01:04:16.800 --> 01:04:19.540
If you want to talk about
that when you stop reading,

01:04:19.540 --> 01:04:22.350
if you don't, if you do
anything along these lines,

01:04:22.350 --> 01:04:25.513
but it came up, I think in
all three of your comments.

01:04:28.390 --> 01:04:30.290
Sonia, do you wanna start?

01:04:30.290 --> 01:04:31.980
you can also take this in
a very different direction

01:04:31.980 --> 01:04:32.813
if you'd like.

01:04:32.813 --> 01:04:33.646
- I'm happy to start.

01:04:33.646 --> 01:04:36.920
And I'm really glad that
you raised us because

01:04:36.920 --> 01:04:40.037
when at one point someone said to me,

01:04:40.037 --> 01:04:42.040
"Oh, artists don't read."

01:04:42.040 --> 01:04:44.299
And I found that so offensive.

01:04:44.299 --> 01:04:48.650
I have a very heavily
research based practice

01:04:48.650 --> 01:04:51.670
and that is a lot of, a lot of texts.

01:04:51.670 --> 01:04:56.440
I last time I had to read,
I had 23 boxes of books

01:04:56.440 --> 01:04:58.686
with a as...

01:04:58.686 --> 01:05:01.620
But books are not the
only relevant researchers

01:05:01.620 --> 01:05:05.020
as Akua was saying like
funerals, the kitchen,

01:05:05.020 --> 01:05:10.020
those places are deep places of learning,

01:05:10.130 --> 01:05:12.670
very humbling spaces of learning.

01:05:12.670 --> 01:05:16.830
And so for me, my research
based practice is, I mean,

01:05:16.830 --> 01:05:18.080
I'm mining that,

01:05:18.080 --> 01:05:21.840
but I'm also thinking
in terms of art history,

01:05:21.840 --> 01:05:24.720
visual history, the
visual language of Europe

01:05:25.610 --> 01:05:30.550
and the visual language of
the continent of Africa,

01:05:30.550 --> 01:05:32.780
some of the things that thread through

01:05:32.780 --> 01:05:34.590
what is carried through to the Caribbean.

01:05:34.590 --> 01:05:36.490
And then I'm also thinking in terms of

01:05:37.650 --> 01:05:39.870
states and here in Europe.

01:05:39.870 --> 01:05:43.162
So all of those spaces are research.

01:05:43.162 --> 01:05:47.060
Some embodied research, like
actually going to a foundation

01:05:47.060 --> 01:05:49.550
or actually being in a great
house in the Caribbean.

01:05:49.550 --> 01:05:50.920
Like my research,

01:05:50.920 --> 01:05:53.307
I feel like my research
practice started when I was 12

01:05:53.307 --> 01:05:55.450
and I was taken to Barrett house,

01:05:55.450 --> 01:05:59.500
which is one of the great
circle, great houses

01:05:59.500 --> 01:06:02.270
around the former plantations in Jamaica.

01:06:02.270 --> 01:06:05.070
And I was taken there
because I was considered

01:06:05.070 --> 01:06:05.940
to be too uppity.

01:06:05.940 --> 01:06:08.330
And it was time to show
me where it came from.

01:06:08.330 --> 01:06:10.577
And so I was standing
at age 12 in this house,

01:06:10.577 --> 01:06:12.910
and that was the beginning of like

01:06:12.910 --> 01:06:14.240
dealing with great houses.

01:06:14.240 --> 01:06:19.240
And I feel like that that kind
of research embodied research

01:06:20.160 --> 01:06:22.420
as well as like book
based re research is like,

01:06:22.420 --> 01:06:25.530
just like, if you're doing
both, it's really tough.

01:06:25.530 --> 01:06:30.000
It's hard work but very meaningful.

01:06:30.000 --> 01:06:35.000
So and I can explain my work
voice in terms of research.

01:06:35.477 --> 01:06:38.820
And I have to, because it's a
very hostile space out there.

01:06:38.820 --> 01:06:39.873
I mean, when I wrote,

01:06:41.030 --> 01:06:45.600
when I wrote about the Hogarth for Tete,

01:06:45.600 --> 01:06:47.360
I had a really bad pushback.

01:06:47.360 --> 01:06:48.193
This is not a...

01:06:48.193 --> 01:06:51.063
How can you say this is a woman?

01:06:53.084 --> 01:06:55.350
Has it come to, this was the cry,

01:06:55.350 --> 01:06:59.073
like even the furniture is implicated.

01:07:01.280 --> 01:07:03.410
So you have to base everything I say,

01:07:03.410 --> 01:07:05.430
like on prestigious research,

01:07:05.430 --> 01:07:10.430
because it's actually
attacking the material culture

01:07:10.440 --> 01:07:12.110
and people don't like that.

01:07:12.110 --> 01:07:14.800
If you are going to say,
"oh, this person is bad,

01:07:14.800 --> 01:07:15.882
they were a slave owner."

01:07:15.882 --> 01:07:18.223
That's not so bad because that's separate.

01:07:19.530 --> 01:07:22.123
But if you attack the material culture,

01:07:23.640 --> 01:07:25.570
we're all implicated.

01:07:25.570 --> 01:07:27.110
Or if you are questioning
the material copy,

01:07:27.110 --> 01:07:30.280
we're all implicated and
that's really problematic.

01:07:30.280 --> 01:07:33.140
And of all the comments, like
many people were invited to,

01:07:33.140 --> 01:07:38.040
to reconfigure Hogarth,
but mine was singled out

01:07:38.040 --> 01:07:40.270
from for considerable more attack

01:07:40.270 --> 01:07:42.453
simply because I was doing that.

01:07:44.300 --> 01:07:49.300
Yeah, so it's necessary if you are doing

01:07:49.610 --> 01:07:53.370
something that's challenging,
I kind of stayed quoted to,

01:07:53.370 --> 01:07:54.258
to be prestigious researcher.

01:07:54.258 --> 01:07:58.560
And I really thank my
first degree for that

01:07:58.560 --> 01:08:01.680
because I was never able
to say anything remotely

01:08:03.940 --> 01:08:08.310
work without backing anything up massively

01:08:09.830 --> 01:08:12.037
in those spaces, so, yeah.

01:08:13.557 --> 01:08:16.450
- I'll just jump and
say, I became a writer.

01:08:16.450 --> 01:08:18.870
I think of myself as a lyrical,

01:08:18.870 --> 01:08:23.340
like a romantic lyricist
within the hip hop tradition.

01:08:26.290 --> 01:08:29.060
I became a writer through reading,

01:08:29.060 --> 01:08:31.080
through being a reader first and foremost.

01:08:31.080 --> 01:08:33.910
I mean, as a little girl,
I read Phillis Wheatley

01:08:33.910 --> 01:08:37.693
Langston Hughes, Maya
Angelo, like, I mean,

01:08:39.020 --> 01:08:41.910
just all of the greats, right?

01:08:41.910 --> 01:08:44.440
Until I started to ask questions,

01:08:44.440 --> 01:08:46.640
I still read Phillis
Wheatley and Langston Hughes,

01:08:46.640 --> 01:08:49.630
but at some point you kind
of become radicalized.

01:08:49.630 --> 01:08:54.630
And then I was looking for some,

01:08:54.850 --> 01:08:56.820
some thin to sharpen my sword,

01:08:56.820 --> 01:08:58.370
to sharpen my oyster knife

01:08:58.370 --> 01:09:02.880
and started reading like
Zora and or Audrey Lord.

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:06.200
And of course the brilliant
genius Bell Hooks.

01:09:06.200 --> 01:09:09.920
And then I discovered this
beautiful pool of black feminist

01:09:09.920 --> 01:09:12.130
literature to which I'm still drowning in.

01:09:12.130 --> 01:09:13.510
I don't want to come up to the surface.

01:09:13.510 --> 01:09:15.390
Don't ask me to come out, I'm here.

01:09:15.390 --> 01:09:19.210
Okay, y'all talking to me at
the bottom at the ocean floor.

01:09:19.210 --> 01:09:23.270
But when I discovered Toni
Morrison is when my life just

01:09:24.200 --> 01:09:28.440
Toni Morrison gave me
a standard and you know

01:09:28.440 --> 01:09:32.940
Like Katia a window to see
if the window jumps through,

01:09:32.940 --> 01:09:34.390
if you know what I mean,

01:09:34.390 --> 01:09:38.691
like a world, but also, like you said,

01:09:38.691 --> 01:09:42.410
reading, writing, speaking, listening,

01:09:42.410 --> 01:09:45.810
and I think like listening, right?

01:09:45.810 --> 01:09:48.370
Like seeing recording,
looking at what's happening.

01:09:48.370 --> 01:09:52.681
Like that's in my opinion,
what's what makes one a good,

01:09:52.681 --> 01:09:54.690
like, if we're talking about hip hop,

01:09:54.690 --> 01:09:57.498
like a good emcee you know,

01:09:57.498 --> 01:10:00.613
like you have to have kind
of like the poets are,

01:10:02.964 --> 01:10:07.964
the profits, like, so you
have to kind of be open

01:10:08.000 --> 01:10:11.120
to some extent you're reading
every anything to like,

01:10:11.120 --> 01:10:14.113
make sense and then to produce,

01:10:15.620 --> 01:10:20.620
our compass to construct a compass for,

01:10:20.633 --> 01:10:23.963
So, yeah.

01:10:28.750 --> 01:10:32.870
- Yeah, I've written
four poetry collections

01:10:32.870 --> 01:10:35.040
and it wasn't until this last one,

01:10:35.040 --> 01:10:38.810
this fourth one that I
really have a note section

01:10:38.810 --> 01:10:43.280
where I reference things that
I've read and researched.

01:10:43.280 --> 01:10:47.130
And I think it's important I guess if,

01:10:47.130 --> 01:10:51.170
if we're writing as I am about
things that actually happened

01:10:52.050 --> 01:10:55.457
historical occurrences, that,
if we want to be authentic

01:10:55.457 --> 01:11:00.260
and we wanna speak truthfully,
I think it's important to,

01:11:00.260 --> 01:11:02.880
to read at least those secondary sources

01:11:04.030 --> 01:11:05.573
and become familiar with them.

01:11:06.900 --> 01:11:08.470
Just if you want to be an ethical,

01:11:08.470 --> 01:11:12.263
I guess, an ethical poet it's
necessary for you to do that.

01:11:14.445 --> 01:11:15.363
Excuse me.

01:11:16.330 --> 01:11:20.567
And yeah, so there was
something else I wanted to say

01:11:20.567 --> 01:11:22.023
and I completely forgot.

01:11:24.670 --> 01:11:26.240
Yeah. I don't what I was gonna say more.

01:11:26.240 --> 01:11:29.600
- Well, I have a question for
you, Rodney, about reading.

01:11:29.600 --> 01:11:33.300
Is there anything to say about
your background in philosophy

01:11:33.300 --> 01:11:35.770
as a student of philosophy
and its relationship

01:11:35.770 --> 01:11:37.783
to the work you do as an artist?

01:11:38.930 --> 01:11:40.342
- Yeah, excuse me.

01:11:40.342 --> 01:11:43.600
A lot of my work is actually informed by

01:11:43.600 --> 01:11:46.130
looking at the world
philosophically, I guess.

01:11:46.130 --> 01:11:50.100
And so, I guess in contemporary poetry,

01:11:50.100 --> 01:11:53.360
maybe shying away from
abstraction is a common thing,

01:11:53.360 --> 01:11:55.122
but I don't do that.

01:11:55.122 --> 01:11:58.023
And especially dealing
with this kind of work.

01:11:59.010 --> 01:12:00.213
And we mentioned before

01:12:00.213 --> 01:12:02.380
that it sometimes gets a bit too heavy

01:12:02.380 --> 01:12:04.580
and sort of removing
yourself through abstraction,

01:12:04.580 --> 01:12:08.010
I think is one technique
that I think I use

01:12:08.010 --> 01:12:11.393
and philosophy comes in handy there.

01:12:13.300 --> 01:12:14.818
And also I think there's,

01:12:14.818 --> 01:12:17.940
there's this sort of
classical clash between

01:12:17.940 --> 01:12:20.621
philosophers and poets, right?

01:12:20.621 --> 01:12:22.500
There're different ways
of approaching the world.

01:12:22.500 --> 01:12:27.380
But I think philosophy is really

01:12:29.219 --> 01:12:33.080
important in a lot of ways
and like the way even the,

01:12:33.080 --> 01:12:35.823
like the poetry that I write at least is,

01:12:37.360 --> 01:12:39.940
like couched I guess in the language,

01:12:39.940 --> 01:12:43.030
because I don't...

01:12:43.030 --> 01:12:46.110
I think if I wanted to do
something else I use fiction or,

01:12:46.110 --> 01:12:50.800
or pros, or something like that,
as opposed to using poetry.

01:12:50.800 --> 01:12:52.230
And so there's something that,

01:12:52.230 --> 01:12:54.710
to be gained in poetry that I think that

01:12:54.710 --> 01:12:57.763
sort of philosophies as
a technique illuminates,

01:12:58.800 --> 01:13:02.400
and it's looking at Emily
Dickinson's famous line about

01:13:02.400 --> 01:13:04.723
looking at the world sort of,

01:13:07.870 --> 01:13:09.240
shoot, and I've forgotten the...

01:13:09.240 --> 01:13:10.410
I've forgotten the quote,

01:13:10.410 --> 01:13:12.180
but looking at it sort of a scan, right?

01:13:12.180 --> 01:13:13.603
Not looking at it directly.

01:13:14.750 --> 01:13:17.372
And so, I mean, I think that you,

01:13:17.372 --> 01:13:22.372
if not overtly it's that
influences there in the background,

01:13:22.920 --> 01:13:25.350
you probably heard it
working in the poems, right?

01:13:25.350 --> 01:13:28.650
It sort of like to write elliptically,

01:13:28.650 --> 01:13:30.400
especially dealing with the heavy,

01:13:30.400 --> 01:13:32.740
heavy stuff in this collection.

01:13:32.740 --> 01:13:33.930
And I seem to be,

01:13:33.930 --> 01:13:37.640
I seem to be tiring out, sorry about that.

01:13:37.640 --> 01:13:38.890
I'm forgetting my quotes.

01:13:40.410 --> 01:13:42.420
- Well, we're almost at time I think.

01:13:42.420 --> 01:13:43.480
Sonia, were you gonna add something?

01:13:43.480 --> 01:13:44.313
Please.

01:13:44.313 --> 01:13:46.080
- I was gonna add that,

01:13:49.070 --> 01:13:51.460
I studied philosophy at St. Andrews,

01:13:51.460 --> 01:13:54.150
I didn't fit Major in Philosophy,

01:13:54.150 --> 01:13:58.560
but it was one of my minor
things that I studied,

01:13:58.560 --> 01:13:59.746
obviously European philosophy.

01:13:59.746 --> 01:14:04.210
And it struck me how often
they would talk of thinking

01:14:04.210 --> 01:14:07.347
in terms of furniture, in
terms of tables and chairs

01:14:07.347 --> 01:14:12.190
and that's really led me to think about

01:14:12.190 --> 01:14:13.950
like that feeds into a lot of my work

01:14:13.950 --> 01:14:15.428
with performing furniture,

01:14:15.428 --> 01:14:20.150
this space of the philosopher
at the table and the chair

01:14:20.150 --> 01:14:22.397
and all the labor that goes
into supporting the philosopher.

01:14:22.397 --> 01:14:25.940
That's not that kind of happens
around that domestic space.

01:14:25.940 --> 01:14:27.983
That's never questioned or theorized.

01:14:31.379 --> 01:14:33.210
After graduation, I had the opportunity

01:14:33.210 --> 01:14:35.465
to really study African philosophy,

01:14:35.465 --> 01:14:39.487
pretty large, but philosophies
of different parts of Africa.

01:14:40.617 --> 01:14:42.500
And that's really informed my work.

01:14:42.500 --> 01:14:45.170
And some of it has come out
only I've discovered how much

01:14:45.170 --> 01:14:50.170
is in the work by actively
seeking relationships with,

01:14:51.830 --> 01:14:53.640
with other artists on the continent

01:14:53.640 --> 01:14:58.640
who then start to explain
to me, this is what this is.

01:14:59.090 --> 01:15:02.400
So it must be a kind of a blood memory

01:15:02.400 --> 01:15:06.123
or some kind of other memory
that's, I'm calling on there.

01:15:06.980 --> 01:15:09.263
And as akua was talking about listening.

01:15:10.330 --> 01:15:12.600
I didn't talk about
this in my presentation.

01:15:12.600 --> 01:15:14.810
I also have a whole thing about,

01:15:14.810 --> 01:15:17.680
I talk about how I listen to furniture.

01:15:17.680 --> 01:15:19.080
So I'm sitting and listening to the chair,

01:15:19.080 --> 01:15:21.340
like, what does the chair want to do?

01:15:21.340 --> 01:15:23.710
So for example, the
gentleman's club chair,

01:15:23.710 --> 01:15:25.990
I spend many days listening to that chair

01:15:25.990 --> 01:15:28.890
and said it wanted to rest itself.

01:15:28.890 --> 01:15:33.890
So there's a deep philosophy
of listening to objects

01:15:36.350 --> 01:15:41.192
that is very productive
a driver in my work.

01:15:41.192 --> 01:15:43.740
And so sometimes I say
I performative practice,

01:15:43.740 --> 01:15:47.070
we also have a listening,
a listening practice.

01:15:47.070 --> 01:15:50.570
And yeah, I think that also comes back to

01:15:50.570 --> 01:15:53.590
philosophy of what can speak
and what is allowed to speak

01:15:53.590 --> 01:15:54.940
and what is considered dumb

01:15:56.520 --> 01:15:58.790
that also undermines like a European,

01:15:58.790 --> 01:16:01.780
Eurocentric philosophy,
whereas other philosophies

01:16:01.780 --> 01:16:04.780
understand that everything has memory

01:16:04.780 --> 01:16:06.753
and everything can speak.

01:16:10.160 --> 01:16:10.993
- Brilliant.

01:16:12.410 --> 01:16:13.710
- Yeah, akua I saw you nodding.

01:16:13.710 --> 01:16:14.550
I wondered if you might wanted...

01:16:14.550 --> 01:16:19.550
- Yes, she's giving me my
entire life, with her words,

01:16:19.580 --> 01:16:22.017
Roberta Flack said, "Killing me Softly."

01:16:23.340 --> 01:16:28.340
Yes, I find it similar in my own practice,

01:16:29.220 --> 01:16:31.523
just listening to the character,

01:16:34.518 --> 01:16:37.257
when the character now is able to speak

01:16:38.610 --> 01:16:40.393
and you kind of just,

01:16:41.360 --> 01:16:43.670
you have to like yield and allow yourself

01:16:43.670 --> 01:16:45.993
to be a vessel for what comes.

01:16:47.060 --> 01:16:49.180
And sometimes afterwards,

01:16:49.180 --> 01:16:51.570
I might look back at something
I've created and it's like,

01:16:51.570 --> 01:16:54.440
I wasn't even there, you know what I mean?

01:16:54.440 --> 01:16:59.440
I don't know if like there
in some spaces there,

01:17:00.160 --> 01:17:02.940
it is not really safe to talk in this way,

01:17:02.940 --> 01:17:05.270
'cause people kind of
think that you're crazy,

01:17:05.270 --> 01:17:06.870
you know what I mean?

01:17:06.870 --> 01:17:09.740
But I do get what Sonia's saying,

01:17:09.740 --> 01:17:12.550
because I do think that
there's an African philosophy

01:17:12.550 --> 01:17:16.960
where that's just understood,
there's space for it

01:17:16.960 --> 01:17:20.420
and like there's vocabulary
and like people understand,

01:17:20.420 --> 01:17:22.870
that you're channeling
something and that's just,

01:17:25.530 --> 01:17:27.483
and that's just like what it is.

01:17:29.480 --> 01:17:30.313
Yeah.

01:17:32.490 --> 01:17:33.660
- I appreciate that so much.

01:17:33.660 --> 01:17:36.410
And I guess as a final
question and I just wanted to,

01:17:36.410 --> 01:17:39.750
again, raise an issue more
than a specific question,

01:17:39.750 --> 01:17:42.830
but see if it prompts you to,

01:17:42.830 --> 01:17:46.563
to any remarks and that's
just something forward looking

01:17:46.563 --> 01:17:49.090
that you've been thinking about
recently, you know, Rodney,

01:17:49.090 --> 01:17:50.747
you mentioned the kind
of state of exception

01:17:50.747 --> 01:17:52.900
and the knife edge that we seem to be on

01:17:52.900 --> 01:17:54.020
or in, at the moment.

01:17:54.020 --> 01:17:59.020
And there are so many
reasons to feel despondency

01:17:59.700 --> 01:18:02.200
and paralysis in this moment.

01:18:02.200 --> 01:18:03.690
And I know that the three of you are

01:18:03.690 --> 01:18:05.440
certainly moving forward in many ways

01:18:05.440 --> 01:18:08.300
and you're watching other people
move forward in many ways.

01:18:08.300 --> 01:18:10.740
So just wanted to invite
you to say something

01:18:10.740 --> 01:18:11.573
along those lines,

01:18:11.573 --> 01:18:13.960
whether it's about something
you're excited about doing

01:18:13.960 --> 01:18:15.620
that you haven't already talked about here

01:18:15.620 --> 01:18:18.621
or something you see
other people doing that's,

01:18:18.621 --> 01:18:21.650
giving you life and joy or something

01:18:21.650 --> 01:18:23.230
along those lines right now.

01:18:23.230 --> 01:18:25.030
It could be a nice way to close if people

01:18:25.030 --> 01:18:28.040
wanna speak in that spirit.

01:18:28.040 --> 01:18:31.897
I mean, there is a kind of
philosophy behind the question,

01:18:31.897 --> 01:18:34.220
which is one that we need one another

01:18:34.220 --> 01:18:36.500
and that we need to be
mindful of the good things

01:18:36.500 --> 01:18:39.150
happening in the world and
take strength in those things.

01:18:39.150 --> 01:18:42.090
And so like, anyway, I would
just invite any comments

01:18:42.090 --> 01:18:45.440
or thoughts that you might
have in that spirit, Rodney.

01:18:45.440 --> 01:18:47.763
- Yeah, I'm really excited about,

01:18:47.763 --> 01:18:52.763
I worked with youth poets
as poet laureate in McAllen.

01:18:53.330 --> 01:18:55.050
And one of the things that we did was

01:18:55.050 --> 01:18:58.770
we published an anthology of youth poets

01:18:58.770 --> 01:19:01.080
from the Rio Grande Valley in Texas.

01:19:01.080 --> 01:19:04.660
And so I'm really excited
about like these younger poets

01:19:04.660 --> 01:19:07.537
that are coming up and the
wonderful, wonderful work

01:19:07.537 --> 01:19:09.320
that they're putting together.

01:19:09.320 --> 01:19:11.350
I think they really have a strong voice,

01:19:11.350 --> 01:19:15.980
I think a lot of them are
really mindful of history

01:19:15.980 --> 01:19:19.116
and really trying to,

01:19:19.116 --> 01:19:21.410
they have these social justice concerns,

01:19:21.410 --> 01:19:23.390
I think that are important.

01:19:23.390 --> 01:19:26.040
And so, they're not only
doing that like on the page,

01:19:26.040 --> 01:19:28.667
but also out in the world and
they're heavily involved in,

01:19:28.667 --> 01:19:31.072
that's just so hopeful to me because

01:19:31.072 --> 01:19:34.610
I grew up in a time where
poetry was heavily controlled

01:19:34.610 --> 01:19:36.990
by a certain type of person, right?

01:19:36.990 --> 01:19:40.210
And so I wasn't welcomed, I
don't think into that world.

01:19:40.210 --> 01:19:42.490
And now there's so much going on

01:19:42.490 --> 01:19:44.740
where poetry is very accepting of,

01:19:44.740 --> 01:19:47.510
people from different walks
of life and people of color.

01:19:47.510 --> 01:19:50.587
And these younger poets I think,
are really embracing that.

01:19:50.587 --> 01:19:52.930
And so that fills me with hope to see

01:19:52.930 --> 01:19:55.240
where the poetry world at least that,

01:19:55.240 --> 01:19:58.690
I know and love is heading
and it's really growing

01:19:58.690 --> 01:20:00.840
and becoming more accepting, so...

01:20:03.270 --> 01:20:06.420
- I'll say that I recently
became a mother and

01:20:12.570 --> 01:20:14.588
it's a masterclass,

01:20:14.588 --> 01:20:17.080
there's so much that I've learned

01:20:17.080 --> 01:20:18.980
that I may have thought of intellectually,

01:20:18.980 --> 01:20:21.953
but like now that it's embodied
and I'm in the fullness,

01:20:22.800 --> 01:20:25.520
it's a masterclass I've learned a lot.

01:20:25.520 --> 01:20:29.110
Like just stretched and
to expand and to widen

01:20:30.300 --> 01:20:33.610
and the depth of my
heart, what it means to,

01:20:33.610 --> 01:20:36.663
to love someone and also to be,

01:20:37.893 --> 01:20:42.893
to be up close to see something so pure

01:20:42.940 --> 01:20:47.940
and that kind of innocence
and excitement so closely,

01:20:49.390 --> 01:20:53.150
that's so beautiful and kind of helps me

01:20:53.150 --> 01:20:58.150
have hope for what's to come.

01:20:58.390 --> 01:21:00.380
And it's also...

01:21:00.380 --> 01:21:02.490
I mean, it's also left me very terrified,

01:21:02.490 --> 01:21:03.410
like in all honesty,

01:21:03.410 --> 01:21:06.459
I've never been more terrified in my life.

01:21:06.459 --> 01:21:09.620
Like I have a little black
boy here, this is a miracle.

01:21:09.620 --> 01:21:13.560
Like, there's a level of terror
that has to send it upon me,

01:21:13.560 --> 01:21:14.877
but I'm not trying to get into that.

01:21:14.877 --> 01:21:16.423
And I wasn't the question,

01:21:17.500 --> 01:21:20.980
but just to now think of a new vocabulary,

01:21:20.980 --> 01:21:23.960
how to explain the complexity
of the world to a child,

01:21:23.960 --> 01:21:28.027
who's seeing things for the
first time, "Oh, look mango."

01:21:29.270 --> 01:21:33.210
To experience that, the
taste of something sweet

01:21:33.210 --> 01:21:36.280
for the first time, this
is really, really nice

01:21:36.280 --> 01:21:40.543
and it's definitely glorious.

01:21:49.416 --> 01:21:51.060
- Hopeful things.

01:21:51.060 --> 01:21:54.023
So in terms of art, I mean today,

01:21:54.023 --> 01:21:57.704
the Venice Spring opened and Simon Le

01:21:57.704 --> 01:22:00.200
and Sonia voice respectively

01:22:00.200 --> 01:22:03.573
are to women of color
opening national pavilions,

01:22:04.550 --> 01:22:07.833
very exciting, 'cause
she followed their work.

01:22:08.940 --> 01:22:11.823
I think that's really
interesting and hopeful.

01:22:13.100 --> 01:22:16.260
In terms of the most
pressing issues of our time,

01:22:16.260 --> 01:22:21.260
which I think are Social injustice,

01:22:21.740 --> 01:22:26.740
which is rooted in slavery,
racism, and the environment,

01:22:28.180 --> 01:22:30.300
which I think I've keep trying to say,

01:22:30.300 --> 01:22:32.280
I think it's just one, this one body,

01:22:32.280 --> 01:22:36.080
this one thing I think that
there's really interesting work

01:22:36.080 --> 01:22:40.850
in terms of reparations not
just in terms of finances,

01:22:40.850 --> 01:22:44.560
but reparations in terms of
environmental reparations

01:22:44.560 --> 01:22:46.963
and putting land back to what it was.

01:22:48.340 --> 01:22:50.550
I think that's really, really exciting.

01:22:50.550 --> 01:22:53.130
There are a number of groups
that are working on that

01:22:53.130 --> 01:22:57.393
and that's for me like
also a hopeful space.

01:23:00.120 --> 01:23:01.500
- Well, thank you so
much all three of you.

01:23:01.500 --> 01:23:02.543
I mean, I'm just,

01:23:04.549 --> 01:23:06.850
it's hard not to sound very cliche here,

01:23:06.850 --> 01:23:09.820
but you know, really
appreciate what you've given me

01:23:09.820 --> 01:23:10.750
in this conversation.

01:23:10.750 --> 01:23:13.070
And you know, I feel like

01:23:13.070 --> 01:23:15.220
just being witness to your commitment,

01:23:15.220 --> 01:23:20.073
to how thoughtful, how
hardworking you are,

01:23:21.210 --> 01:23:25.470
how insightful you are, how
collaborative you are in your,

01:23:25.470 --> 01:23:27.993
in your many spheres of collaboration,

01:23:29.250 --> 01:23:31.700
certainly bolsters me.

01:23:31.700 --> 01:23:34.100
I mean, I think there's many reasons to

01:23:34.100 --> 01:23:36.240
be upset and be worried
and we would be wrong

01:23:36.240 --> 01:23:39.820
not to be upset and
not to be very worried,

01:23:39.820 --> 01:23:42.450
but I think the leadership
and the vision that,

01:23:42.450 --> 01:23:44.353
that three of you and
others whom you know,

01:23:44.353 --> 01:23:49.040
and are working with provide
is just, it's important to me.

01:23:49.040 --> 01:23:51.733
So thank you all, all three of you.

01:23:54.080 --> 01:23:56.550
Really, again, I'll just reiterate how

01:23:56.550 --> 01:23:58.880
honored we've been to have
an association with you

01:23:58.880 --> 01:24:01.781
and how grateful we are to you.

01:24:01.781 --> 01:24:06.781
How closely we'll be keeping
track of you in the good ways,

01:24:08.939 --> 01:24:11.900
not in the surveillance
place, in the good ways,

01:24:11.900 --> 01:24:14.700
in the years to come
and how eager we are to,

01:24:14.700 --> 01:24:16.040
to continue to support your work.

01:24:16.040 --> 01:24:18.170
So thank you to the three of you,

01:24:18.170 --> 01:24:19.840
thank you to our hidden staff.

01:24:19.840 --> 01:24:23.650
And thank you to those
of you here on the call,

01:24:23.650 --> 01:24:26.910
who are with us today and we wish you,

01:24:26.910 --> 01:24:29.440
all good things and the
weeks and months ahead and,

01:24:29.440 --> 01:24:31.260
and are grateful again for your time.

01:24:31.260 --> 01:24:32.500
And Sonia, I know you're in a

01:24:32.500 --> 01:24:34.040
very different time zone than ours.

01:24:34.040 --> 01:24:38.880
So it's another time of
day or night where you are

01:24:38.880 --> 01:24:40.400
than it is for the rest of us.

01:24:40.400 --> 01:24:42.380
So thank you all and be well,

01:24:42.380 --> 01:24:45.103
and we'll look forward to
staying in touch in the future.

